course: dictionary
lesson: 5688
title: Lockdown: one word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «In a lockdown drill you stay silent, follow the teacher and keep away from doors and windows until told it is over.» Here lockdown is a school practice for danger outside, not a whole country staying at home.
explain: The same word can also mean a time when people must stay at home and many places close, to stop a disease spreading. «During lockdown, schools closed and lessons went online.» Ask yourself what situation the sentence describes, then pick the meaning that fits.
explain: Look for clues. Words like drill, emergency and silent point to a school safety practice. Words like closed, online and apart point to a time when a disease was spreading. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Lockdown is a noun, and you can talk about a lockdown or several lockdowns.
fact: «In lockdown» and «go into lockdown» mean that people or places are shut in and kept safe.
fact: A «lockdown drill» is practice for staying inside, with doors locked, in case there is danger outside.
fact: During the COVID-19 pandemic, many countries had lockdowns: schools closed and people kept apart.
fact: Drills are practice, not a sign that something bad is coming, and real emergencies are very rare.
exercise: «In a lockdown drill you stay silent and keep away from doors and windows.» What is a lockdown drill? | A party where everyone locks the doors | A lesson about doors and windows | A way of closing shops for a holiday | *Practice for staying safe inside when there may be danger outside
exercise: «During lockdown, schools closed and lessons went online.» Which meaning of lockdown fits here? | A type of lock for a school gate | A day when everyone goes on a trip | A safety practice with the teacher in the classroom | *A time when people must stay at home to stop a disease spreading
exercise: Which word shows that lockdown drills are practice, not a real danger? | emergencies | *practise | schools | knows
test: Ella reads: «Real emergencies are very rare, so schools practise lockdowns.» What does lockdown most likely mean here? | A type of rain | *A safety drill when everyone stays inside with doors locked | A kind of dinner served at lunchtime | A trip to the seaside
test: «The city was in lockdown, and the shops stayed shut for weeks.» What does in lockdown mean? | The shops are very busy | The city has a new park | *People must stay at home and many places are closed | Everyone is going on holiday
exam: Jayden says lockdown only means a school drill. Is he right, and how do you know from the context? | Yes, it only ever means a school drill | *No, it can also mean a time when people must stay at home and many places close | Yes, because drills are always about fire | No, it means a new kind of school bag
